## Why This Question Is Asked
This question assesses practical memory management skills and understanding of lock-free data structures—essential competencies for systems programming and performance-critical applications.

## Key Concepts
- **Lock-free free-list design**: Using an atomic head pointer enables concurrent allocation/deallocation without locks
- **Memory layout**: Fixed-size 64-byte blocks with the next pointer as the first field ensure proper alignment on 64-bit systems
- **ABA problem**: Tagged pointers (using the unused bits in a 64-bit pointer) can prevent ABA issues in high-contention scenarios
- **Contention testing**: Multi-threaded stress tests verify correctness under concurrent access patterns

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Block {
    struct Block* next;
    unsigned char payload[56];
} Block;

static _Atomic(Block*) free_head;

Block* allocate(void) {
    Block* head;
    Block* new_head;
    do {
        head = atomic_load_explicit(&free_head, memory_order_acquire);
        if (!head) return NULL;
        new_head = head->next;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
        &free_head, &head, new_head,
        memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed));
    return head;
}

void free_block(void* ptr) {
    Block* block = (Block*)ptr;
    Block* head;
    do {
        head = atomic_load_explicit(&free_head, memory_order_acquire);
        block->next = head;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
        &free_head, &head, block,
        memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed));
}
```

## Test Plan (4 Threads)
1. **Initialization**: Preallocate all N blocks, verify free_head points to the full list
2. **Basic correctness**: Single-threaded allocate/free sequences, verify all blocks returned
3. **Concurrent stress**: 4 threads repeatedly allocate and free blocks; track allocation counts to ensure no leaks or double-frees
4. **Boundary conditions**: Allocate until empty, verify NULL returned; free all blocks, verify full recovery
5. **Data integrity**: Write patterns to payload, free, allocate fresh blocks, verify previous data is not present

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of fundamental thread synchronization primitives, blocking behavior, and race-condition avoidance in practical concurrent data structures. It demonstrates a candidate's ability to implement classic producer-consumer patterns safely.

## Key Concepts
- **Circular buffer:** Fixed-capacity queue using modulo arithmetic for wraparound
- **Mutual exclusion:** Single mutex protecting all shared state (head, tail, count)
- **Blocking semantics:** Condition variables to suspend threads when queue is full/empty
- **Spurious wakeups:** Why `wait()` must be called with a predicate in a while loop
- **Fairness:** FIFO ordering from condition variable wakeups and serialized mutex access

## Code Example
```cpp
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <vector>

template<typename T>
class BoundedBlockingQueue {
  static constexpr size_t N = 1024;
  std::vector<T> buf_{N};
  size_t head_ = 0, tail_ = 0, count_ = 0;
  std::mutex mutex_;
  std::condition_variable not_full_, not_empty_;

public:
  void push(T item) {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    not_full_.wait(lock, [this] { return count_ < N; });
    buf_[tail_] = std::move(item);
    tail_ = (tail_ + 1) % N;
    ++count_;
    not_empty_.notify_one();
  }

  T pop() {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    not_empty_.wait(lock, [this] { return count_ > 0; });
    T item = std::move(buf_[head_]);
    head_ = (head_ + 1) % N;
    --count_;
    not_full_.notify_one();
    return item;
  }
};
```

## How It Works

A 2MB arena is divided into 64-byte blocks, yielding 32768 blocks. The free list is a single atomic head packed into a 64-bit value: [ version (upper 32 bits) | head index (lower 32 bits) ]. A free block stores the index of the next free block. On init, all blocks form a chain starting at 0, ending with a sentinel (0xFFFFFFFF).

The allocator uses a lock-free approach:
- Alloc: read the head with acquire, compute the next pointer, CAS to update head to next and increment version. Return the block corresponding to the popped index.
- Free: write the freed block’s next pointer to current head, CAS to push it back and increment version.

ABA is mitigated by the version counter; each successful CAS increments the version, so a stale head cannot be mistaken for a newer one.

Memory order choices:
- Loads use memory_order_acquire to synchronize with the producer/consumer.
- Stores use memory_order_release or memory_order_acq_rel to ensure visibility of the updated head and block linkage.

### Key Points
- **64-bit head encoding**: version guards against ABA by differentiating sequential head values.
- **Block linkage stored in arena**: next indices reside in the block headers themselves.
- **Initialization**: pre-link 32768 blocks and set head to 0 with version 1.
- **Threading model**: works with many producers/consumers with minimal contention due to CAS on a single head.

### Example
```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>

#define ARENA_SIZE (2*1024*1024)
#define BLOCK_SIZE 64
#define BLOCKS (ARENA_SIZE / BLOCK_SIZE)

typedef struct { uint32_t next; } Block;
static uint8_t arena[ARENA_SIZE];
static _Atomic uint64_t head; // [version:upper32 | index:lower32]

static inline void init_slab(void) {
  for (uint32_t i = 0; i < BLOCKS-1; ++i) ((Block*)arena)[i].next = i+1;
  ((Block*)arena)[BLOCKS-1].next = 0xFFFFFFFFu;
  atomic_store_explicit(&head, ((uint64_t)1 << 32) | 0u, memory_order_relaxed);
}

void* slab_alloc(void) {
  uint64_t oldv, newv;
  uint32_t idx, ver, next;
  while (1) {
    oldv = atomic_load_explicit(&head, memory_order_acquire);
    idx = (uint32_t)(oldv & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    ver = (uint32_t)(oldv >> 32);
    if (idx == 0xFFFFFFFFu) return NULL;
    next = ((Block*)arena)[idx].next;
    newv = ((uint64_t)(ver+1) << 32) | (uint64_t)next;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(&head, &oldv, newv,
                                            memory_order_acquire, memory_order_relaxed))
      return (void*)(arena + (size_t)idx * BLOCK_SIZE);
  }
}

void slab_free(void* p) {
  if (!p) return;
  uint32_t idx = ((uint8_t*)p - arena) / BLOCK_SIZE;
  uint64_t oldv, newv;
  while (1) {
    oldv = atomic_load_explicit(&head, memory_order_acquire);
    uint32_t ver = (uint32_t)(oldv >> 32);
    ((Block*)arena)[idx].next = (uint32_t)(oldv & 0xFFFFFFFFu);
    newv = ((uint64_t)(ver+1) << 32) | idx;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(&head, &oldv, newv,
                                            memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed))
      break;
  }
}

